WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.069 --> 00:00:12.230
My next tudor was Rob Resnick, and Rob went
to MIT. He would come over, make sure I got

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:16.990
my math and my english done. He also became
like a big brother, which was really good.

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:21.720
He would take me over to MIT where I liked
to throw the baseball. He would take me over

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.619
to his frat house, where I seen the Dallas
Cowboys and the Green Bay Packers play -- one

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:30.470
of the first football games I got to watch
on TV. Simply because of the fact that my

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:37.700
mother didn't let me watch a lot of TV. The
students. When I first came 40 years ago,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:48.129
when my father died, that's when I came into
the business, the MIT students were ... slovenly

00:00:48.129 --> 00:00:58.500
attired. They had hair down to their knees,
some of them, they didn't comb it, some of

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:11.390
them didn't wash, most of them didn't shave.
They were pretty gross at times. As soon as

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:20.140
women came to the MIT campus, all of the sudden
it was a huge difference in the men. And they

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:28.280
took showers, and they shaved, and they cleaned
up. They were much nicer to be around. An

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:36.549
interesting dichotomy there between the old
MIT kids and the new ones. And I think it's

00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:43.290
about 50 percent women now. Well, of course
people who are involved in that kind of stuff

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:50.049
are a little bit more idealistic than the
average, I don't know about the average student.

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:56.969
It's not like it was in the late 60s, early
70s when it was it was a massive amount of

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:04.020
social consciousness. The students were interested,
in those days, in us, as if we were members

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:12.070
of the same species. But normally they don't
think that way. Most of the kids who come

00:02:12.070 --> 00:02:16.510
through Central Square, they act like you're
not there, you're like pigeons or something

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:21.199
like that. And I suppose if I went there that's
what I would think about them. But the ones

00:02:21.199 --> 00:02:26.669
I knew, I still know some of them -- I've
made some good friends. And they still keep

00:02:26.669 --> 00:02:39.609
in touch with me. Just a few. There's a reputation,
of course, that MIT students are very diligent,

00:02:39.609 --> 00:02:45.139
they're much more serious, and maybe of a
different class background than the ones at

00:02:45.139 --> 00:02:53.340
Harvard. Harvard has a reputation of frivolous,
wealthy students. Whether it's justified or

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:58.510
not I don't know. But that's what people think
when they think of the two.

